<h1>Live PA Setup — ANIMAL</h1>
<p><strong>Goal:</strong> Professional live PA set with real-time remixing, looping, sample firing, DJ-style performance</p>
<p><strong>Current gear:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Ableton Live (version TBC)</li>
<li>Push 2</li>
<li>Mini mixer (brand/model TBC)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2>Core Setup: Ableton + Push 2 Live PA</h2>
<p>This is already a legit professional rig. Plenty of touring artists run this exact combo. Here&#39;s how to dial it in properly.</p>
<hr>
<h2>1. Ableton Session View — Your Stage</h2>
<p>Live PA lives in <strong>Session View</strong>, not Arrangement. Think of it as a grid of infinite possibilities.</p>
<p><strong>Organize your set like this:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Column (Track)</th>
<th>What goes there</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Drums / Beats</td>
<td>Drum Racks with your core patterns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Bass</td>
<td>Bass loops, synth basslines</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Chords / Pads</td>
<td>Chord progressions, atmospheric pads</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Melody / Lead</td>
<td>Hooks, leads, melodies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Vocals / Samples</td>
<td>Vocal chops, one-shots, FX hits</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>FX Bus</td>
<td>Returns: reverb, delay, effects chain</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Master</td>
<td>Limiter, EQ, meters</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Clip colors:</strong> Color-code by energy level (green = low/intro, yellow = mid, red = peak/drop). You&#39;ll navigate by feel in the dark.</p>
<p><strong>Follow Actions:</strong> Set clips to auto-advance — great for building tension without having to manually launch every loop.</p>
<hr>
<h2>2. Push 2 — Your Instrument</h2>
<p>Push 2 is the hands-on brain of the setup. Key modes to master:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Session Mode:</strong> Launch clips and scenes. Arm/disarm tracks. This is your main performance view.</li>
<li><strong>Note Mode:</strong> Play melodic parts live on the pads. Great for improvised solos, fills.</li>
<li><strong>Drum Rack Mode:</strong> Fire samples, one-shots, and drum patterns on the 4x4 pad grid.</li>
<li><strong>Device Mode:</strong> Twist knobs to control macros, filters, effects in real-time. Map your most-used parameters here.</li>
<li><strong>Loop Mode:</strong> Set and adjust loop start/end points on the fly.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key workflow: Macro Mapping</strong>
Set up macro knobs on your main tracks mapped to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Filter cutoff (for DJ-style filtering)</li>
<li>Reverb send</li>
<li>Delay send</li>
<li>Pitch/transpose</li>
<li>Volume/Gain</li>
</ul>
<p>You&#39;ll turn one knob on Push and the sound changes dramatically.</p>
<hr>
<h2>3. Looping Setup</h2>
<p>For live looping into your set:</p>
<p><strong>Option A: Ableton Looper (recommended)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Drop a Looper device on a dedicated track</li>
<li>Map record/overdub/play/clear to Push 2 buttons</li>
<li>Layer up to 8 loops live — bass, melody, vocals, whatever</li>
<li>Sync to master tempo automatically</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Option B: Clip recording in Session View</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Arm a track → record a clip live → it loops automatically</li>
<li>More flexible than Looper for multi-track live recording</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Best practice:</strong> Set up 4–6 dedicated &quot;live loop&quot; tracks pre-armed and ready. During performance you just hit record on Push and play.</p>
<hr>
<h2>4. Sample Firing</h2>
<p><strong>Drum Rack is your best friend:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Load one-shots, FX hits, vocal chops, risers, drops into Drum Rack</li>
<li>Each pad on Push 2 fires a different sample</li>
<li>Velocity sensitive — hit harder for louder/harder hits</li>
<li>Chain multiple Drum Racks per genre/set section</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Hot tips:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Keep a dedicated &quot;FX hits&quot; rack: white noise risers, downlifters, crowd noise, sirens, stabs</li>
<li>One-shot samples don&#39;t need to be looped — set clip to &quot;trigger&quot; mode, not loop</li>
<li>Color-code your pad grid by type (drums = red, vocals = blue, FX = yellow)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2>5. The Mini Mixer — Where It Fits</h2>
<p>Depends on what you&#39;ve got:</p>
<p><strong>If it&#39;s a DJ mixer (e.g., Pioneer DJM-S3, Rane, Allen &amp; Heath):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Route Ableton&#39;s main output into Channel 1</li>
<li>Use the mixer EQ for live DJ-style cuts (kill the bass, sweep the filter)</li>
<li>Crossfader if you want to blend between Ableton and external source (CDJ, phone, USB)</li>
<li>Run a second source into Channel 2 for mixing in records/tracks on top of your live PA</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>If it&#39;s a compact audio mixer (e.g., Behringer Xenyx, Mackie Mix):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Use it mainly for summing outputs and sending to PA/monitors</li>
<li>Less useful for performance — the EQ and routing is limited</li>
<li>Consider upgrading to a proper DJ mixer for more performance options</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Either way:</strong> Make sure your audio interface is in the signal chain properly — Ableton → interface → mixer → PA.</p>
<hr>
<h2>6. Audio Interface (Critical If You Don&#39;t Have One)</h2>
<p>If your mini mixer doesn&#39;t have a built-in audio interface, you need one between the Mac and the mixer. Without it you&#39;re running through the Mac&#39;s built-in audio which is garbage for live performance.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Focusrite Scarlett 2i2</strong> (~$120) — simple, reliable, 2 outputs + headphone monitoring</li>
<li><strong>Audient iD4 mk2</strong> (~$130) — slightly better converters, same use case</li>
<li><strong>Native Instruments Komplete Audio 6</strong> (~$150) — 6 outputs, great if you need multiple send channels</li>
</ul>
<p>Push 2 connects via USB — doesn&#39;t need the interface, goes straight to Mac.</p>
<hr>
<h2>7. Ableton Link — When to Use It</h2>
<p>Ableton Link syncs tempo wirelessly between devices over WiFi. Use it when:</p>
<ul>
<li>Running a second Mac or iPad alongside Ableton</li>
<li>Using iPad apps like Modstep, Koala Sampler, or GarageBand as a second instrument</li>
<li>Syncing visuals (Resolume, TouchDesigner) to your music</li>
<li>Jamming with another musician who also runs Ableton</li>
</ul>
<p>For a solo live PA, Link is optional — you&#39;re the only clock source. But if you ever play with visual artists or other producers, it&#39;s essential.</p>
<hr>
<h2>8. Signal Chain (The Right Order)</h2>
<pre><code>Mac running Ableton Live
    ↓ (USB)
Push 2 ←→ Mac (bidirectional USB)
    ↓
Audio Interface (USB → Mac)
    ↓
Mini Mixer / DJ Mixer
    ↓
PA System / Monitors
    ↓ (headphone out of mixer)
Headphones (for monitoring)
</code></pre>
<hr>
<h2>9. What to Add Down the Road</h2>
<p><strong>Priority 1 — Push 3 ($800–$1,500)</strong>
Push 3 has a built-in audio interface AND can run standalone (no Mac required). Run your entire live PA from Push 3 alone. Major upgrade for live performance reliability — nothing crashes if your Mac freezes.</p>
<p><strong>Priority 2 — Better DJ Mixer (~$300–$600)</strong>
Something with proper 3-band EQ kill switches, filter knob, and a clean crossfader. Allen &amp; Heath Xone:23 or Pioneer DJM-S3 are solid. This opens up actual DJ mixing technique on top of your live PA.</p>
<p><strong>Priority 3 — MIDI Footswitch (~$60–$150)</strong>
Leaves your hands free for Push while your feet control scene launches or looper record/stop. Keith McMillen SoftStep 2 or Behringer FCB1010 are popular.</p>
<p><strong>Priority 4 — iPad as Second Controller (~$400+)</strong>
Run TouchAble Pro or Lemur on an iPad connected to Ableton via Ableton Link or OSC. Second screen of knobs and faders you can customize for your set. Great for a more hands-on visual display while Push handles the pads.</p>
<p><strong>Priority 5 — Hardware Synth (~$300–$700)</strong>
Adding a hardware synth (Arturia MicroBrute, Roland JD-Xi, Korg Minilogue) gives you a physical instrument to play live. Route it into your mixer alongside Ableton. Changes the whole vibe — sounds rawer, more human.</p>
<hr>
<h2>10. Practice Workflow for a Live Set</h2>
<ol>
<li>Build your track library in Session View — at least 30–40 clips per set</li>
<li>Group clips into &quot;scenes&quot; that represent set energy levels (warm-up, build, peak, drop, outro)</li>
<li>Map your 8 macro knobs on Push before every show — filter, reverb, delay are essential</li>
<li>Practice &quot;launching without looking&quot; — you should be able to navigate your set in the dark</li>
<li>Always have a safety net: a pre-arranged backup set in Arrangement View for if things go sideways</li>
<li>Soundcheck: verify audio interface clock, buffer size (use 256 samples for live, not 128 — less CPU stress), and check latency compensation</li>
</ol>
<hr>
