<hr>
<h2>Option C — The Dream Rig (No Budget Limit)</h2>
<p>This is what a touring festival headliner would spec out. Every piece chosen for maximum performance, reliability, and wow factor.</p>
<hr>
<h3>DJ Core — ~$7,000</h3>
<p><strong>2x Pioneer CDJ-3000</strong> (~$2,400 each = $4,800)
The industry gold standard. Every major club and festival stage on earth has these. Playing CDJ-3000s means you can walk up to any club setup in the world and feel at home. 9-inch touchscreens, Hi-Res audio playback, real jog wheel feel, cloud library sync.</p>
<p><strong>Pioneer DJM-A9</strong> (~$2,500)
Pioneer&#39;s flagship 4-channel mixer. The cleanest analog path of any DJ mixer made. Built-in sound card, 4-band EQ with kill switches, flexible routing, send/return effects loop for hardware FX. This is what plays at Berghain, Fabric, and every A-list venue.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Live Production — ~$3,500</h3>
<p><strong>Ableton Push 3 Standalone</strong> (~$1,500)
Runs your entire Ableton set without a laptop. Built-in audio interface, MPE-capable pads, standalone mode means if your Mac dies the Push keeps going. This is the brain of your live PA.</p>
<p><strong>Ableton Live Suite</strong> (~$750 if not already on it)
Full plugin suite — Max for Live, all instruments and effects. No limits.</p>
<p><strong>Roland SP-404 MK2</strong> (~$500)
Hardware sampler for tactile, hands-on sample firing. Loaded with your FX hits, vocal chops, one-shots. No screen, no menus — just pads and knobs. Connects to your mixer via a dedicated channel. Sounds different from software samples — warmer, more physical.</p>
<p><strong>Teenage Engineering OP-1 Field</strong> (~$1,800)
The wildcard. Portable all-in-one synthesizer, sampler, tape recorder. Unique sounds you can&#39;t get anywhere else. Perfect for adding unexpected live moments — a melody, a texture, a sample grabbed from the room. Feeds into its own mixer channel.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Audio Interface — ~$3,000</h3>
<p><strong>RME Fireface UFX+</strong> (~$3,000)
The absolute best audio interface made for live performance. Rock solid drivers — never crashes, never glitches. 94 I/O channels, ultra-low latency, runs standalone with USB drive if the Mac dies. Overkill for most — perfect for you.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Effects Processing — ~$1,500</h3>
<p><strong>Pioneer RMX-1000</strong> (~$700)
Dedicated DJ performance effects unit. Connects to your DJM-A9 via send/return. Scene FX, Isolate FX, X-Pad for sweeping effects live. Completely separate from software — never crashes.</p>
<p><strong>Eventide H9 Max</strong> (~$700)
The best hardware effects unit made. 52 algorithms — reverb, delay, modulation, pitch shift, looping. MIDI-controlled from Ableton or Push. Adds warmth and depth no plugin can touch.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Control Surface — ~$1,500</h3>
<p><strong>iPad Pro 13-inch + Apple Pencil</strong> (~$1,300)
Running <strong>Lemur</strong> or <strong>TouchOSC</strong> — fully custom touchscreen control surface for Ableton. Design your own interface: faders, XY pads, buttons, anything. Second visual brain alongside Push 3.</p>
<p><strong>Keith McMillen SoftStep 2</strong> (~$200)
MIDI footswitch with 10 pressure-sensitive pads. Hands-free scene launching, looper control, effect bypass while both hands are on gear. Screws to the floor of your stage rig.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Visuals — ~$3,500</h3>
<p><strong>Second MacBook Pro + Resolume Avenue</strong> (~$3,000 + $500 software)
Dedicated visuals Mac running Resolume synced to Ableton Link. Real-time generative visuals that react to your music — beat-matched flashes, waveform animations, custom video loops. Outputs to LED wall or projection. This is what separates a live PA from a live show.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Monitoring — ~$1,500</h3>
<p><strong>Beyerdynamic DT 1770 Pro</strong> (~$500)
Best closed-back headphones for live DJ monitoring. Isolates stage noise, accurate, comfortable for 4-hour sets.</p>
<p><strong>Yamaha HS8 Studio Monitors</strong> (~$1,000/pair)
For your personal booth monitoring. Accurate, flat response. Hear exactly what the PA is doing.</p>
<hr>
