<h3>How It All Connects</h3>
<pre><code>CDJ-3000 (Deck 1) ──┐
CDJ-3000 (Deck 2) ──┼──► DJM-A9 (Channels 1 &amp; 2)
SP-404 MK2 ─────────┤
OP-1 Field ──────────┤
                     │
Push 3 / Ableton ────┼──► RME Fireface UFX+ ──USB──► DJM-A9 (Channel 3 or 4)
                     │
RMX-1000 ────────────┼──► DJM-A9 Send/Return
Eventide H9 Max ─────┘

DJM-A9 Master Out ──► PA System
DJM-A9 Booth Out ───► Yamaha HS8 Monitors
DJM-A9 Headphone ───► Beyerdynamic DT 1770 Pro

iPad Pro (Lemur) ─────────────────────► Ableton (OSC/MIDI over WiFi)
SoftStep 2 (USB) ─────────────────────► Ableton
Ableton Link (WiFi) ──────────────────► CDJ-3000 (tempo sync)

MacBook Pro (Resolume) ──Ableton Link──► Synced visuals to PA/LED wall
</code></pre>
<h3>What Makes This Different</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nothing depends on one device.</strong> CDJs run standalone. Push 3 runs standalone. If the Mac dies, the show continues.</li>
<li><strong>Every sound source has its own channel.</strong> Ableton, SP-404, OP-1 — all independent, all blendable live.</li>
<li><strong>Hardware effects</strong> on top of software effects — the RMX-1000 and H9 Max add warmth and physicality no plugin can replicate.</li>
<li><strong>Visuals are live and reactive</strong> — not a pre-rendered loop, but generative art responding to your music in real time.</li>
<li><strong>Full Ableton Link ecosystem</strong> — everything locked together. Change tempo on the CDJ, everything follows.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Updated: March 14, 2026</em></p>
<hr>
<h2>Option D — The Smart Build (~$10,000–$11,000)</h2>
<p>Same capabilities as the dream rig. Smart substitutions that cut price nearly in half without sacrificing performance.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Where the Money Goes</h3>
<p><strong>Denon SC Live 4</strong> (<del>$3,200) — replaces the two CDJ-3000s + DJM-A9 (</del>$7,300)
One unit does what three pieces did. Standalone, 4 channels, touchscreens on each deck, Ableton Link native, WiFi streaming. Honestly competes with the CDJ setup in most real-world performance scenarios. Saves ~$4,100.</p>
<p><strong>Ableton Push 3 Standalone</strong> (~$1,500) — same as dream rig. Non-negotiable.</p>
<p><strong>Roland SP-404 MK2</strong> (~$500) — same as dream rig. Keep it.</p>
<p><strong>Ableton Live Suite</strong> (~$750) — same. Keep it.</p>
<p><strong>RME Babyface Pro FS</strong> (<del>$800) — replaces RME Fireface UFX+ (</del>$3,000)
Same legendary RME reliability and driver stability. Compact, USB, handles everything you need for a live PA at this level. The UFX+ is overkill for a solo live PA — the Babyface Pro FS is what you actually need. Saves ~$2,200.</p>
<p><strong>Pioneer RMX-1000</strong> (~$700) — same as dream rig. Keep it. Plugs into SC Live 4 send/return.</p>
<p><strong>iPad Pro 11-inch + Lemur</strong> (<del>$900) — replaces iPad Pro 13-inch (</del>$1,300)
Same functionality, smaller footprint, easier to fit on stage. Saves ~$400.</p>
<p><strong>Keith McMillen SoftStep 2</strong> (~$200) — same as dream rig. Keep it.</p>
<p><strong>Use your existing Mac for Resolume</strong> — replaces second MacBook Pro (<del>$3,000)
Run Resolume Avenue (</del>$500) on your current Mac alongside Ableton. Use a USB-C to HDMI adapter to output visuals to a second screen, projector, or LED wall. Tighter on CPU but totally manageable if your Mac is modern. Saves ~$3,000.</p>
<p><strong>Beyerdynamic DT 770 Pro</strong> (<del>$150) — replaces DT 1770 Pro (</del>$500)
Same Beyerdynamic build quality, slightly less accurate but still excellent for live monitoring. Saves ~$350.</p>
<p><strong>Yamaha HS5 Monitors</strong> (<del>$400/pair) — replaces HS8 (</del>$1,000)
Same Yamaha HS family, slightly smaller drivers. Plenty for booth monitoring. Saves ~$600.</p>
