<h3>What You Give Up vs Option C</h3>
<ul>
<li>No CDJ-3000 feel — SC Live 4 jog wheels are good but not the same as CDJ</li>
<li>No OP-1 Field — lose the wildcard instrument (add it later as a want)</li>
<li>No Eventide H9 — lose hardware effects depth (software fills most of this gap)</li>
<li>Tighter CPU headroom if running Ableton + Resolume on same Mac</li>
<li>Slightly less accurate headphone monitoring</li>
</ul>
<h3>What You Keep</h3>
<ul>
<li>Full standalone capability (SC Live 4 + Push 3 both standalone)</li>
<li>Ableton Link tempo sync across everything</li>
<li>Hardware sample firing (SP-404)</li>
<li>Real-time synced visuals (Resolume)</li>
<li>RME reliability for audio</li>
<li>Hands-free foot control (SoftStep)</li>
<li>Custom iPad control surface (Lemur)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Best Add-Ons Later (in order of priority)</h3>
<ol>
<li>OP-1 Field (~$1,800) — when budget allows, adds the most unique capability</li>
<li>Eventide H9 Max (~$700) — hardware effects depth</li>
<li>Second MacBook for dedicated Resolume (~$2,000 refurb) — frees up CPU</li>
<li>CDJ-3000 pair + DJM-A9 — if you ever need club-standard gear for touring</li>
</ol>
<p><em>Updated: March 14, 2026</em></p>
<hr>
<h2>Live PA Best Practices — SOPs from Pro Performers</h2>
<p>Based on how touring artists like Porter Robinson, deadmau5, Flume, RL Grime, Four Tet, and mashup/open-format live performers structure their rigs and workflows.</p>
<hr>
<h3>1. The Golden Rules</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Never touch a mouse on stage.</strong> Everything you need must be mapped to a physical control. If you&#39;re clicking, you&#39;ve already lost.</li>
<li><strong>Build redundancy into everything.</strong> Two of anything critical — laptop backup, audio interface backup, the full set on a USB if Ableton dies.</li>
<li><strong>The set should run without you if needed.</strong> Follow Actions handle the transitions. You&#39;re adding to it, not holding it together.</li>
<li><strong>Color everything.</strong> In a dark venue at 2 AM, you navigate by color, not by reading.</li>
<li><strong>Warp every clip before show day.</strong> Never warp on stage.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3>2. Ableton Set Architecture — How the Pros Build It</h3>
<p><strong>The Track Layout (left to right):</strong></p>
<pre><code>[DRUMS] [PERC] [BASS] [CHORDS] [LEAD/MELODY] [VOCALS] [FX HITS] [LOOPS] [MASTER FX]
</code></pre>
<p>Each column is one sound type. Each row of clips = one &quot;moment&quot; or section of your set.</p>
<p><strong>Color coding by energy (non-negotiable):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>🟢 Green = intro / low energy / rebuild</li>
<li>🟡 Yellow = mid energy / verse / groove</li>
<li>🔴 Red = peak / drop / climax</li>
<li>🔵 Blue = breakdown / emotional moment</li>
<li>⚪ White = transitions / one-shots / stabs</li>
</ul>
<p>You scan the grid by color, not by reading text. In the dark, under pressure, this saves you.</p>
<p><strong>Scene naming convention:</strong></p>
<pre><code>01_INTRO
02_BUILD_1
03_DROP_A
04_BREAKDOWN
05_DROP_B_PEAK
06_OUTRO
</code></pre>
<p>Number them. If you ever want to jump sections, you know exactly where you&#39;re going.</p>
<hr>
<h3>3. Sample Organization — Where Everything Lives</h3>
<p><strong>Folder structure (do this before you build anything):</strong></p>
<pre><code>/Live Set Samples/
├── Drums/
│   ├── Kicks/
│   ├── Snares/
│   ├── Hi-Hats/
│   └── Full Loops/
├── Bass/
│   ├── Sub Bass/
│   ├── Synth Bass/
│   └── Bass Loops/
├── Chords + Pads/
├── Melodies + Leads/
├── Vocals/
│   ├── Full Phrases/
│   ├── Chops/
│   └── Ad-libs/
├── FX/
│   ├── Risers/
│   ├── Downlifters/
│   ├── Impacts/
│   ├── White Noise/
│   └── Crowd FX/
├── Stabs + Hits/
├── Mashup Elements/
│   ├── By Song Title/
│   └── Acapellas/
└── Stems/
    └── By Track Name/
</code></pre>
<p><strong>The key insight from top performers:</strong> Everything is pre-labeled with <strong>key and BPM</strong> in the filename.</p>
<pre><code>kick_heavy_01.wav → kick_heavy_01_128bpm.wav
vocal_chop_yeah_Gm.wav
riser_white_8bar_130bpm.wav
</code></pre>
<p>You never have to guess if something will fit. You see it at a glance.</p>
<hr>
<h3>4. The Drum Rack Setup (SP-404 or Ableton)</h3>
<p>Every pro live PA has a dedicated pad bank for performance hits. Organize it like this:</p>
<p><strong>Pad layout (4x4 grid):</strong></p>
<pre><code>[RISER 2bar] [RISER 4bar] [RISER 8bar] [IMPACT HIT]
[DOWNLIFT]   [WHITE NOISE] [CROWD YEA] [AIRHORN]
[VOCAL CHOP1][VOCAL CHOP2][VOCAL CHOP3][VOCAL STAB]
[KICK SHOT]  [SNARE SHOT] [CLAP SHOT] [808 BOOM]
</code></pre>
<p>Top row = FX and transitions (reach for these on every drop)
Second row = more FX hits
Third row = vocal one-shots for mashup moments
Bottom row = drum one-shots for fills and emphasis</p>
<p><strong>Color code the pads to match the grid colors above.</strong> Red pads = big impacts. Blue pads = smooth/vocal. Green pads = transitions.</p>
<hr>
<h3>5. Macro Mapping — The Eight Knobs That Run Everything</h3>
<p>Every Ableton Group Track gets 8 macros. Map them the same way on every track so muscle memory takes over:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Knob</th>
<th>Function</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>Filter Cutoff</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>Filter Resonance</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>Reverb Send</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>Delay Send</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>Volume</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
<td>Distortion / Drive</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7</td>
<td>Pitch / Transpose</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8</td>
<td>LFO Rate / Chop Speed</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Filter cutoff on Knob 1 is the DJ move — cut the bass out, sweep the filter up, bring it back. You&#39;ll use this constantly.</p>
<hr>
<h3>6. Live Loop Workflow (Looping on the Fly)</h3>
<p>Set up 4–6 &quot;capture tracks&quot; — always armed, always ready.</p>
<p><strong>How pros use them:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Pre-set loop length (4 bars, 8 bars) before the moment</li>
<li>Hit record → play something live (on Push, on a synth, sing into a mic)</li>
<li>Loop fires automatically in sync with the master clock</li>
<li>Stack layers on top — bass loop, then melody loop, then vocal loop</li>
<li>Clear them one by one for a dramatic strip-down breakdown</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Key:</strong> Set Follow Actions on loop clips to &quot;loop again&quot; indefinitely. They keep going until you manually stop them. No babysitting.</p>
<hr>
<h3>7. Mashup-Specific Workflow (Your Lane)</h3>
<p>For open-format mashup live PA (Girl Talk, Crooklyn Clan style), the workflow is different from pure electronic PA.</p>
<p><strong>How it works:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Build &quot;blocks&quot; not full songs. Each block is 16–32 bars of a mashup moment.</li>
<li>Stack the acapella of Song A over the instrumental of Song B — pre-built, pre-warped, in the clip grid.</li>
<li>Launch blocks in real time. Transition with an FX hit or riser between them.</li>
<li>Keep 3–4 blocks at the same energy level so you can extend a peak if the crowd is going nuts.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The secret weapon:</strong> Stems. Strip your tracks down to stems (drums, bass, top) instead of full mixes. Lets you swap drums from one song under the vocals of another live — real-time remixing nobody else in the room can do.</p>
<p><strong>Stem tools:</strong> Moises, Spleeter, or the stems feature in Rekordbox/Virtual DJ.</p>
<hr>
<h3>8. Day-Of Show SOP (Run This Every Time)</h3>
<p><strong>2 hours before:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Open set, verify all samples load (orange missing files = disaster)</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Check all MIDI mappings still assigned (they sometimes drift after updates)</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Verify audio routing — Ableton out to correct interface channels</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Load the same set on backup laptop</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Charge everything with a battery</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Soundcheck:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Play a clip on every track, confirm it comes through PA correctly</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Check headphone mix — can you hear what you need to hear?</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Verify Ableton Link locked to Virtual DJ (if using both)</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Run through your opening 2 minutes cold — exactly what you&#39;ll do when you walk on</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>At the gig:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Buffer size: 256 samples minimum (512 if older Mac). Not 128 — too risky live.</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Turn off WiFi notifications, Do Not Disturb on</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Close every app except Ableton and Virtual DJ</li>
<li><input disabled="" type="checkbox"> Set Mac to never sleep, screensaver off, display brightness down</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Backup plan:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Full pre-arranged set in Arrangement View — if Session View falls apart, hit spacebar and it plays a complete set automatically. Nobody in the audience knows.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3>9. How the Rig Talks to Itself (Integration Map)</h3>
<pre><code>Virtual DJ (tracks/DJ set)
    ↓ Ableton Link (tempo)
Ableton (live production, loops, samples)
    ↓ MIDI
Push 3 (performance control — clips, pads, knobs)
    ↓ MIDI
MIDI controller (channel levels, mutes, faders)
    ↓ MIDI
SoftStep (hands-free scene launch, looper)
    ↓ OSC/WiFi
iPad Lemur (visual macro control, custom faders)

All audio ──► Audio Interface ──► DJ Mixer ──► PA
Resolume ──► Ableton Link ──► Synced visuals
</code></pre>
<p>Everything is on one clock. Change tempo anywhere and it propagates everywhere. That&#39;s the whole game.</p>
<hr>
<h3>10. The Mental Model — How to Think About a Live Set</h3>
<p><strong>Before the show:</strong> You&#39;re a programmer. Build blocks, warp samples, map controls. No improvisation here — this is prep work.</p>
<p><strong>During the show:</strong> You&#39;re a musician AND a DJ. The structure is your safety net. Everything else is reaction — reading the room, extending peaks, dropping to a breakdown when people need to breathe.</p>
<p><strong>The pro move:</strong> Build more than you&#39;ll use. 40 scenes for a 60-minute set. You&#39;ll use 25 of them. The other 15 are there if the crowd tells you to go somewhere you didn&#39;t plan.</p>
<p><em>Updated: March 14, 2026</em></p>
<hr>
<h2>SICKICK Reference — Live PA Setup &amp; Workflow</h2>
<p>SICKICK (Canadian DJ/producer) is one of the closest existing models to what ANIMAL is building — live remixing, mashups, original production layered over known tracks, all happening in real time in front of an audience.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Known Gear (from studio videos and live footage)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>MacBook Pro</strong> — main brain, runs Ableton</li>
<li><strong>Ableton Live Suite</strong> — Session View, heavy macro use</li>
<li><strong>Ableton Push 2</strong> — clip launching, pad performance, macro control</li>
<li><strong>2x Pioneer CDJ-2000NXS2</strong> — track playback, acapellas, stems</li>
<li><strong>Pioneer DJM-900NXS2</strong> — main mixer, where Ableton and CDJs meet</li>
<li><strong>iPad</strong> — secondary control surface, custom layout</li>
<li><strong>Additional MIDI controllers</strong> — varies by session/show</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3>How He Structures a Performance</h3>
<p><strong>The core concept:</strong> He doesn&#39;t play a pre-built set. He loads a well-known track (or its stems/acapella) and builds a live remix around it on the fly in front of the audience. The performance IS the production.</p>
<p><strong>CDJs handle:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Full track playback</li>
<li>Acapellas of the song being remixed</li>
<li>Stems (when available)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Ableton handles:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>His original drums, bass, synths, and loops</li>
<li>Effects processing on the incoming CDJ signal</li>
<li>Live clip launching and looping</li>
<li>All production layers underneath the vocal/original</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Pioneer mixer handles:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Blending CDJ and Ableton channels together</li>
<li>EQ manipulation live (kill bass, sweep filter)</li>
<li>Send/return effects</li>
<li>The master output to PA</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3>His Pre-Built Sound Library</h3>
<p>This is the key to making it look effortless — he&#39;s not building from scratch on stage. Before every show:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Massive sample library organized by key and vibe</strong> — drums, bass hits, synth stabs, chord loops, FX</li>
<li>All clips are pre-warped and key-tagged</li>
<li>He knows his library cold — when he hears a vocal, he already knows which bass loop fits in that key</li>
<li><strong>Pre-built production &quot;sketches&quot;</strong> — loose arrangements in Ableton that work across multiple songs in the same key/tempo range</li>
</ul>
<p>The live performance is assembly, not creation. The creation happened in the studio. On stage he&#39;s just snapping pieces together faster than the audience can track.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Macro Strategy</h3>
<p>Push 2 macros mapped to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Filter cutoff on incoming CDJ channel (routed through Ableton)</li>
<li>Reverb throw on vocals</li>
<li>Delay feedback</li>
<li>Pitch/transpose on loops</li>
<li>Stutter/beat repeat rate</li>
</ul>
<p>He&#39;s constantly tweaking these — the filter sweeps, reverb throws, and stutter effects are what make it feel live and human rather than a playback set.</p>
<hr>
<h3>The Workflow Loop (What&#39;s Actually Happening)</h3>
<pre><code>1. Load track/acapella on CDJ → play into mixer
2. Identify key and tempo → match Ableton session
3. Launch bass loop + drum loop on Push → blends under the acapella
4. Add production layers one at a time (builds tension)
5. Drop — big moment, everything hits together
6. Strip layers back → let the original vocal breathe
7. Transition into next track/acapella
8. Repeat — each &quot;song&quot; is 2–4 minutes of live remix
</code></pre>
<hr>
<h3>What You Can Steal From His Approach</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Build your library first.</strong> Before you perform, you need 500+ pre-warped, key-tagged clips. That library IS your instrument.</li>
<li><strong>Learn 20 songs cold.</strong> Know exactly which of your elements fit over the 20 tracks you&#39;ll likely remix. No searching during the show.</li>
<li><strong>The filter sweep IS the performance.</strong> Half of what makes his sets look impressive is just aggressive filter and reverb manipulation in real time. Master that one move and everything else follows.</li>
<li><strong>Acapellas are gold.</strong> The more acapellas you have pre-warped and ready, the more live remix moments you can create on demand.</li>
<li><strong>React to the room.</strong> He stays on a moment if the crowd responds — extends it, layers more. If something isn&#39;t landing, he moves. Read the room, not your setlist.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h3>Where to Study His Setup</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>YouTube: SICKICK channel</strong> — has multiple studio walkthrough and live session videos</li>
<li>Look specifically for his &quot;remixing in the studio&quot; series — shows his actual Ableton layout and workflow</li>
<li>Instagram Live sessions — raw, unedited, shows real-time decision making</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3>The ANIMAL Advantage</h3>
<p>SICKICK built his reputation on remixing other people&#39;s music live. You have everything he has PLUS:</p>
<ul>
<li>25+ years of actual DJ performance experience (reading crowds, timing, energy management)</li>
<li>Crooklyn Clan mashup pedigree — you&#39;ve been doing the concept longer than it was called a live PA</li>
<li>Your own original music catalog to pull from (Kade, Madison, Aria) — not just other people&#39;s tracks</li>
<li>Deep genre versatility — EDM, hip-hop, pop, rock all in one set</li>
</ul>
<p>The setup is the same. The instincts are already there. It&#39;s just about getting the rig dialed in.</p>
